    title: "\"Scapegoat\"",
    date: "coined 1530",
    tier: "DOCUMENTED",
    tags: ["biblical-phrases"],
    body: "Coined by William Tyndale in his 1530 translation of Leviticus 16, for the goat that symbolically carried Israel's sins into the wilderness on the Day of Atonement ('escape goat' for Hebrew 'azazel' — whose exact meaning is itself debated: 'the goat that departs' vs. a proper name). From that translation choice, English gained its universal word for a person unfairly blamed for others' faults. Entered common secular usage by the 19th century. One man's translation decision in 1530 is in your mouth today.",
    deep: "The paper trail here is unusually clean. In 1530 William Tyndale published his English translation of the Pentateuch, working from the Hebrew rather than only from the Latin Vulgate. In Leviticus 16 he met the phrase 'la-azazel' — the destination or recipient of the second goat in the Day of Atonement ritual, the one not sacrificed but sent alive into the wilderness bearing the people's confessed sins. Earlier translators had already struggled with the word: the Greek Septuagint rendered it with a phrase meaning roughly 'the one sent away', and Jerome's Latin gave 'caper emissarius', the emissary goat. Tyndale followed the interpretive tradition that read the Hebrew as 'the goat that departs' and coined a new English word for it: the '(e)scape goat'. The King James translators of 1611 kept his coinage, as they kept a very large share of his phrasing, and that is how the word entered the permanent stock of English.\n\nWhat remains genuinely unsettled is the Hebrew behind it. 'Azazel' appears in the Bible only in this chapter, and three main readings have serious support. Rabbinic tradition, preserved in the Mishnah and Talmud tractate Yoma, treats it as a place — a harsh cliff in the wilderness from which the goat was cast down in Second Temple practice. The ancient Jewish book of 1 Enoch treats Azazel as the proper name of a fallen angel or wilderness demon, and many modern scholars think Leviticus likewise presupposes a named being of the wilderness to whom the sin-laden goat is dispatched. A third, older line of interpretation — the one Tyndale inherited — parses the word as 'the goat that goes away'. Each reading has real arguments; none is proven. So the English word rests on one specific resolution of a Hebrew puzzle that is still open, which is worth labeling honestly: the coinage is documented fact, the etymology of the underlying Hebrew is debated.\n\nThe semantic afterlife is also well documented. For centuries 'scapegoat' remained tied to the ritual; the Oxford English Dictionary's dated citations show the figurative sense — a person made to carry blame that belongs to others — becoming common in the nineteenth century, and the verb 'to scapegoat' and the noun 'scapegoating' are essentially twentieth-century developments, taken up by psychology and sociology to describe how groups off-load guilt and hostility onto a target. Later theorists built whole accounts of religion and violence around the mechanism, but that is interpretation layered on top of the word, not part of its origin. The core claims of this entry sit at the strongest evidence level: a datable book, a nameable translator, an unbroken citation trail from 1530 to the present.",
