    tags: ["biblical-phrases"],
    body: "From Daniel 5: during Belshazzar's feast, a hand writes 'MENE MENE TEKEL UPARSIN' on the palace wall, announcing Babylon's fall to the Persians — which is documented history (539 BC, Cyrus; and note: critics long claimed 'Belshazzar' was fictional until cuneiform records confirmed him as crown prince and co-regent under Nabonidus, a detail Daniel gets right that later Greek historians didn't know). The phrase entered everyday English after the KJV spread the story, and now means any clear omen of coming doom that people refuse to see.",
    deep: "The scene comes from Daniel 5, written in Aramaic: at a great feast, King Belshazzar drinks from vessels looted from the Jerusalem temple, a disembodied hand writes on the plaster, and Daniel is summoned to read the words no one else can interpret. The inscription itself is a chain of weight-and-money terms — mene (a mina), tekel (a shekel), and peres (a half-measure) — which Daniel unpacks as a triple wordplay: numbered, weighed, divided, with 'peres' also punning on 'Persians'. That same night, the text says, Babylon falls. The fall of Babylon to Cyrus's forces in 539 BC is independently documented, above all in the Nabonidus Chronicle, a Babylonian cuneiform record.\n\nThe Belshazzar detail is the striking evidential point. For much of the nineteenth century critics treated him as an invention, because the Greek historians who wrote about Babylon — Herodotus and Xenophon — never mention him, and the known last king of Babylon was Nabonidus. Then cuneiform documents recovered from Mesopotamia, including a cylinder inscription of Nabonidus from Ur, named Bel-shar-usur as the king's eldest son, and other texts show Nabonidus spending years away from the capital while his son exercised royal authority there. So Daniel preserves a genuine piece of sixth-century political detail that had dropped out of the later Greek tradition. Honesty requires the other side too: Daniel calls Belshazzar 'king' outright and calls Nebuchadnezzar his 'father', which scholars reconcile in various ways (co-regency; 'father' as royal predecessor in Semitic idiom), and the date and composition history of the book of Daniel as a whole are a separate, genuinely contested question that this entry does not settle. The DOCUMENTED grade here attaches to the fall of Babylon in 539 BC and to Belshazzar's existence and status — both confirmed by contemporary records — not to every detail of the feast narrative, which no outside source describes.\n\nAs an English idiom, the phrase is a child of the translated Bible. The story circulated for centuries in Latin and in art — Rembrandt's painting of Belshazzar's feast, with the glowing Hebrew letters, is the most famous image — but it was the vernacular Bibles culminating in the King James Version that put the scene in every English-speaking household, and 'the writing (or handwriting) on the wall' gradually detached from the story to become a free-standing figure of speech for doom that is announced in advance and ignored. Dictionaries of quotations and the OED trace the figurative use back through the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries; the precise first figurative use is not something anyone can pin to a single dated sentence, and this entry does not pretend to.",
