    title: "\"By the skin of my teeth\", \"a drop in the bucket\", \"eat, drink and be merry\"",
    date: "texts c. 6th–3rd century BC; English via 1611",
    tier: "DOCUMENTED",
    tags: ["biblical-phrases"],
    body: "Three of dozens of everyday phrases that are direct Bible quotations most speakers no longer recognize: 'skin of my teeth' is Job 19:20 (a bare-escape image from the Bible's oldest poetry); 'a drop in the bucket' is Isaiah 40:15 (the nations, next to God); 'eat, drink and be merry' merges Ecclesiastes 8:15 with Jesus' parable of the rich fool (Luke 12:19 — where the phrase is the FOLLY, a context modern usage inverted). Others in daily use: 'go the extra mile' (Matthew 5:41 — referring to the documented Roman law letting soldiers impress civilians to carry loads one mile), 'salt of the earth' (Matthew 5:13), 'the powers that be' (Romans 13:1, Tyndale's phrasing), 'a labor of love' (1 Thessalonians 1:3), 'the blind leading the blind' (Matthew 15:14). English speakers quote this book daily without knowing it.",
    deep: "Each of these phrases has a checkable address. 'By the skin of my teeth' renders Job 19:20, where Job describes escaping with nothing left; the Hebrew is obscure — teeth have no skin, and commentators have argued for centuries over whether the image means the gums, the barest surface, or something now lost — and the English translators' decision to render it literally is precisely what made it memorable. The wording is in the 1560 Geneva Bible before the King James Version carried it to dominance. 'A drop in the bucket' is Isaiah 40:15, where the nations of the earth are weighed against God and found to be a drop from a bucket and dust on scales. 'Eat, drink and be merry' fuses two texts: Ecclesiastes 8:15, where enjoying food and drink is commended as God's gift amid life's vapor, and Luke 12:19, where a rich man says almost exactly these words to himself the night before he dies — in the parable the sentence is the emblem of folly, so modern cheerful usage quotes the punchline of a warning as if it were advice.\n\n'Go the extra mile' preserves a piece of Roman administrative reality. Matthew 5:41 uses a technical Greek verb, angareuo, borrowed ultimately from the Persian royal courier system, for the legal right of soldiers and officials to impress civilians to carry loads a fixed distance. The same verb appears when Simon of Cyrene is 'compelled' to carry Jesus' cross (Mark 15:21). Jesus' instruction — if conscripted for one mile, go two — only makes sense against that documented practice, which is attested in papyri and Roman-era regulations governing requisition. 'The powers that be' is William Tyndale's 1526 phrasing of Romans 13:1, kept by the KJV; 'salt of the earth', 'a labor of love', and 'the blind leading the blind' likewise passed from the translations into ordinary speech, their sources forgotten.\n\nThe scale of this borrowing has been measured. The linguist David Crystal, in his book Begat: The King James Bible and the English Language, went idiom by idiom and counted 257 expressions in modern English that trace to the King James Bible — while also showing, against the popular version of the claim, that the KJV coined relatively few of them itself: most were inherited from Tyndale, the Geneva Bible, and earlier translations, and the KJV's role was transmission at unmatched scale. Nothing here is speculative; the texts are ancient, the translations are dated and printed, and the citation chains sit in historical dictionaries. What is genuinely uncertain is confined to details inside the source texts, like the exact Hebrew sense behind Job's teeth — the pathway into English is documented end to end.",
