    title: "\"God bless you\" after a sneeze",
    date: "custom ancient; Christian form attributed to 590 AD",
    tier: "TRADITION",
    tags: ["customs"],
    body: "Responding to sneezes with a blessing is documented as ALREADY ancient in Roman times — Pliny the Elder (1st century AD) asks why we salute sneezes, and notes the emperor Tiberius demanded it; ancient Greek sources treat sneezes as omens. The specific Christian story — Pope Gregory I ordering 'God bless you' during the 590 AD plague of Rome — is attributed by later tradition, not contemporary record. So: the custom itself is documented and pre-Christian; the famous plague origin-story is tradition. Most cultures have an equivalent (German 'Gesundheit' = health; Hebrew 'labriut' = to your health; Arabic responses bless the sneezer) — a near-universal human custom whose deep origin (sneeze as omen? soul? health fear?) is genuinely unknown.",
    deep: "Two different claims live inside this custom, and they sit at two different evidence levels. The first claim — that people have saluted sneezes since antiquity — is documented. In Homer's Odyssey, Telemachus's loud sneeze is taken by Penelope as a favorable omen, which shows the sneeze already carrying supernatural freight in archaic Greece. The Aristotelian Problemata puzzles over why the sneeze, alone among bodily expulsions, is treated as something sacred. And Pliny the Elder, writing his Natural History in the first century AD, asks outright why we salute sneezes and records that the emperor Tiberius — no soft touch for superstition — insisted on the courtesy even while riding in his carriage. So by Roman times the custom was old enough that an encyclopedist treated its origin as a lost puzzle. That point matters: the practice predates Christianity and cannot have been invented by any pope.\n\nThe second claim is the famous origin story: that during the plague that struck Rome around 590 AD, Pope Gregory I ordered short blessings said over sneezers, sneezing being taken as a symptom of the disease, and that our 'God bless you' descends from this decree. Here the evidence thins out exactly as the TRADITION grade warns. The plague itself is historical — it carried off Gregory's predecessor Pelagius II — and near-contemporary sources describe Gregory leading penitential processions through the stricken city. But the specific detail about blessing sneezes is not found in the contemporary record; it surfaces in later retellings and is repeated from popular book to popular book without an early source. It may preserve a genuine memory; it may be a medieval or modern back-formation that attached a famous name to an existing habit. There is no way to verify it, which is what 'tradition only' means on this site.\n\nThe deepest layer — why humans bless sneezes at all — is genuinely unknown, and honest folklore reference works say so. The candidate explanations are all recorded as folk beliefs somewhere: the sneeze as omen needing a counter-word; the soul momentarily expelled or the heart momentarily stopped, requiring protection; the sneeze as first symptom of illness, requiring a health-wish. That last logic is visibly encoded in the many languages whose response is a health formula rather than a divine blessing — German 'Gesundheit', Hebrew 'labriut', the Romance 'salute'/'salud', while Arabic custom blesses and answers the sneezer with formulas of praise. But recorded folk explanations are not origins; they are the stories cultures tell about a habit they inherited. The custom is documented for well over two thousand years; its birth, and Gregory's role in the Christian form of it, remain beyond the evidence.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Pliny the Elder, Natural History 28.5 (custom already old, 1st c. AD)", url: null, where: "Pliny, Natural History, book 28 — the passage on saluting sneezes and Tiberius; in the Loeb edition or the Bostock–Riley translation available in classics libraries and standard online classics collections" },
      { cite: "Aristotle, Problemata (sneeze as omen)", url: null, where: "The Aristotelian Problemata, section on the nose and sneezing; Loeb Classical Library or the Oxford revised translation of Aristotle" },
      { cite: "Gregory I attribution: later medieval tradition, no contemporary source", url: null, where: "Negative finding: not in Gregory's own writings or near-contemporary accounts of the 590 plague; the attribution circulates in later retellings — see folklore reference works such as Opie & Tatem, A Dictionary of Superstitions" }
    ]
