    title: "\"Honeymoon\"",
    date: "attested 1546",
    tier: "DOCUMENTED",
    tags: ["customs", "myth-check"],
    body: "First attested in English in 1546 ('hony moone'), and 16th-century writers themselves explain it: married affection, sweet at first, wanes like the moon — a wry metaphor, not a ritual. The widely repeated claim that it comes from an ancient Babylonian custom of drinking honey-mead for a month after the wedding has no ancient source behind it — no Babylonian text records any such custom; it's a modern just-so story. The wedding-trip meaning ('bridal tour') developed in the 19th century.",
    deep: "The word walks into the record in 1546, in the era of John Heywood's printed collection of English proverbs, spelled 'hony moone'. What makes this origin unusually secure is that we do not have to guess what sixteenth-century speakers meant by it, because they told us. Within a few years of the first attestation, Richard Huloet's English dictionary of 1552 defined the term and explained the image: newlyweds' affection is exceedingly sweet at first and then, like the moon, it wanes — and common people call that first sweet season the honey moon. In other words, the earliest users understood it as a wry, slightly cynical metaphor about the trajectory of married love: honey for the sweetness, moon for the waning. There is no ritual, no drink, and no month in the early evidence — just a joke about how marriages cool.\n\nThe popular counter-story deserves explicit labeling: folk etymology. The claim that 'honeymoon' descends from an ancient custom — usually pinned on Babylon, sometimes on the Norse or the Teutonic peoples — in which the couple drank honey wine or mead for a full moon's cycle after the wedding has no ancient source behind it. No Babylonian text records a mead-month; Assyriologists have not produced one, and the story's tellers never cite one, because the trail always dead-ends in other popular retellings. Mead-drinking at weddings certainly happened in various cultures, which gives the story its surface plausibility, but plausibility is not evidence of derivation, and the dated English record — metaphor first, explained by contemporaries as metaphor — leaves the mead story nothing to explain. This is a textbook case of a just-so story invented after the fact to make a word feel more ancient and ceremonial than it is.\n\nThe later semantic history is documented in the dictionary record. Through the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries 'honeymoon' meant the early harmonious period of a marriage (and, extended, of any new relationship — political honeymoons included). The meaning most people reach for first today, the post-wedding trip, grew out of the nineteenth-century fashion for the 'bridal tour', in which couples of means traveled after the wedding; the word transferred from the period to the journey. Other European languages carry the same image — French 'lune de miel', for instance — through borrowing and calquing of the shared metaphor. What remains genuinely uncertain is small and typical of word history: 1546 is the earliest surviving attestation, not necessarily the moment of coinage, and the word may have circulated in speech before it reached print. The origin as a waning-moon metaphor, though, rests on the explicit testimony of the earliest sources themselves.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "OED, 'honeymoon' (1546 first citation)", url: null, where: "OED entry 'honeymoon' (library access) — dated citations from 1546 and the sense development from 'first sweet period' to 'wedding trip'" },
      { cite: "John Heywood's Proverbs (1546); Richard Huloet (1552, explains the waning-moon sense)", url: null, where: "Heywood's proverb dialogue and Huloet's Abecedarium Anglico-Latinum; both quoted in the OED entry and in standard phrase-history references" },
      { cite: "Absence of any Babylonian mead-month source", url: null, where: "Negative finding: no cuneiform text attests such a custom; the claim circulates only in modern popular retellings without citation — compare any Assyriological handbook on Mesopotamian marriage" }
    ]
